1.  Introduction

    FPL90 is a programming language based on FORTRAN. A FPL90 program
is to be preprocessed by the FPL90 preprocessor to produce a fortran
program.  We describe here some of the main features of the FPL90.
   1) One can write a program in a completely goto-less and label-less
      form.  Block structure of the algorithm can be transparently
      manifested in the source program.
   2) The preprocessor itself is written compactly in FPL90 ( ca. 1700
      source cards ): thus, a user can easily add a new feature to the
      preprocessor.
   3) Simple parameterless internal procedures can be defined within a
      fortran subprogram.  Recursive call of internal procedures is
      permitted if so declared.
   4) Simple macros may be defined.
   5) Output listing is automatically indented according to the block
      structure of the source code.

2.  FPL90 Source Structure

2.1  Source Card Format
    A FPL90 source program may be written in any position between
columns 2 and 72. It may contain blank cards. Columns 73 to 80 may be
used for card identification. If the last character in a line is not a
"semicolon", the FPL7 preprocessor supplies a semicolon symbol before
processing.
2.2  Continuation
    Two consecutive - symbols, i.e. "--" is regarded as a continuation
symbol.  The rest of the contents of the card is ignored, and the FPL90
preprocessor reads the next card logically connected as a single line.
2.3  Comment
    The "slash" symbol at the top of a logical line indicates that the
following text is a comment until a "semicolon" symbol is encountered.
Comment line which starts with the symbol "/*" is copied as a comment
card to the output Fortran statements.

3.  Syntax of the FPL90

    We use the following conventions to describe the FPL90 syntax.
   1) Words enclosed by the symbols "<" and ">" represents the elements
      of the FPL90 syntax. The definitions are given in this section.
   2) Words enclosed by the symbols "<<" and ">>" should be interpreted
      from the text. It usually refer to Fortran type statement or
      concept.
   3) The "or" symbol "!" separates alternatives. One and only one of
      the alternatives should be chosen from the list. "null" in an
      alternative list indicates that the selection is optional.
   4) The square brackets "[" and "]" are used to indicate grouping.
      An alternative list is, when neccessary, enclosed by them.
   5) The other letters and symbols should be written as it stands.

   ex.   [A!B];([null!C])       means one of the followings.
           A;()
           A;(C)
           B;()
           B;(C)

<FPL90 program>   := <program unit>
                  ! <FPL90 program> ; <program unit>

<program unit>   := [ <process option> ; ! null ]
                    [ PROGRAM ! SUBROUTINE ! FUNCTION ]
                    <<subprogram name>> [ (<<parameter list>>) ! null ];
                    [<declaration part> ; ! null ]
                    [ BEGIN ! DO ] ; <block> ;
                    [ <local procedures> ; ! null ]

<process option> := PROCESS <option list>

         ex.     PROCESS LIST NO MAP

<option list>    := <option>  ! <option list> <option>

<option>         := [ NO ! null ] [ LIST ! MAP ]
                  ! HEADDER <<headder>>
                  ! FILE    [ <<file name>> ! '<<file name>>' ! / ]
                  ! INCLIB  [ <<file or directry name>> !
                             '<<file or directry name>>' ]
         note 1   LIST     FPL source printing by the preprocessor
                  MAP      printing of internal procedures
                  HEADDER  set headder of FPL source printing
                  FILE     specify fortran source file name
         note 2   FILE option controls output file name of fortran
                  source code. If FILE option is not used, file name
                  assumes default file name.
                  1. default   File name is taken from firstly
                     encountered program or subprogram name. ".FOR" is
                     added to it to make a full name.
                  2. <<file name>>    In this case, ".FOR" is added
                    to "file name". Only an identifier is allowed for
                    this type of specification.
                  3. '<<file name>>'  Full file name can be specified
                    by enclosing file name with "'" symbol.
                  4. /   Current output fortran file is closed if there
                    was any, and new file assumes default file name
                    ( see case 1.).                          
         note 3   INCLIB option specifies macro inclusion file or directry
                  name. If INCLIB option is not used, file or directry name
                  assumes default value.
                  ( For MS DOS )
                  1. default   Directry name is the current directry. 
                    ".INC" is added to it to make a full file name, i.e.,
                       file  = .\<<macro name>>.INC
                  2. <<directry name>>    In this case, 
                       file  = <<directry name>>\<<macro name>>.INC
                    Only an identifier is allowed for this type of
                    file specification.
                  3. '<<directry name>>'  Any directry name can be
                    specified by giving directry name as a character
                    string.
                   ( For main frame )
                  4. default   File name is FPLLIB.INCL, i.e.,
                       file  = FPLLIB.INCL(<<macro name>>)
                  5. <<file name>>    In this case, 
                       file  = <<file name>>(<<macro name>>)
                    Only an identifier is allowed for this type of
                    file specification.
                  6. '<<file name>>'  Any directry name can be
                    specified by giving directry name as a character
                    string.

<declaration part>
                 := <<declaration statement>>
                  ! <declaration part> ; <<declaration statement>>
         ex.
         IMPLICIT INTEGER * 4 (A-Z)
         INTEGER * 4    --
         N,      --       CURRENT ROW OF THE QUEEN.
         NS,     --       SIZE OF THE BOARD (DEFAULT  8).
         TSOL,   --       TOTAL NUMBER OF SOLUTIONS (DEFAULT 10).
         POS(20); /       COLUMN POSITION OF THE QUEEN IN EACH ROW.
         INTEGER  IQ9999, IQ9998(0:300); /  stack of the return address
         DATA     IQ9999 /0/;            /  initial pointer

<block>          := <statement list> ; END

<statement list> := <statement> ! <statement list> ; <statement>

<local procedures>
                 := <procedure> ! <local procedures> ; <procedure>

<procedure>      := PROCEDURE <procedure name> [ ; RECURSIVE ! null ] ;
                    <block>

         note     "RECURSIVE" should be added for a procedure which
                  may be called recursively. Since the procedure is
                  parameterless, a user is entirely responsible for the
                  generation control of the variables.
                  Return address of the procedure call is stacked in
                  an integer array IQ9998 and stack pointer name is
                  IQ9999. Thus a user must provide those variables as

                  INTEGER   IQ9999, IQ9998(300)
                  in each subprogram if it contains a recursive internal
                  procedure or in a common block as

                  INTEGER  IQ9999, IQ9998(1000)
                  COMMON /RECURS/ IQ9999, IQ9998

<statement>      := <<FORTRAN statement which does not start with
                     the reserved words of FPL90 with or without a
                     statement number ( at most three digits )>>
                  ! <if statement>
                  ! <for statement>
                  ! <while statement>
                  ! <until statement>
                  ! <exec statement>
                  ! <exit statement>
                  ! <read-write statement>
                  ! <list statement>
                  ! <put statement>
                  ! <list specification>
                  ! <fortran statement>
                  ! <do block>
                  ! <do for block>
                  ! <do while block>
                  ! <do until block>
                  ! <do case arithmetic block>
                  ! <do case character block>
                  ! <do case logical block>
                  ! <do fortran block>
                  ! <variable modification>

         note     List of FPL90 reserved word
                  DO,      IF,      END,     FOR,     PUT,
                  ELSE,    EXEC,    EXIT,    LIST,    THEN,
                  BEGIN,   WHILE,   UNTIL,   BINARY,  FORMAT
                  FORTRAN, PROCESS

<logical expression>
                  := <<fortran logical expression>>
                   ! <<fortran logical expression in which .EQ.,
                     .GT.,.LT., .NE., .GE., .LE., .NOT., .AND., AND
                     .OR. are replaced by =, >, <, ^=, >=, <=,
                     ^, & and symbol "!" respectively>>

         ex.      One can write     A <= B ! X ^= Y instead of
                               A .LE. B .OR. X .NE. Y

<if statement>   := IF <logical expression>
                    [ ; ! THEN ! ; THEN ] <statement>
                    [ null ! ; ELSE <statement> ]

         ex.  1   IF A > B                       ( ; default semicolon )
                  THEN C = D                     ( ; default semicolon )
                  ELSE C = E

         ex.  2   IF A > B THEN C = D

<for statement>  := FOR <<do loop specification>> ; <statement>

         ex.      FOR I = 1, N ; A(I) =  0

         note.    This statement is converted to a fortran DO-loop.
                  A call to a internal procedure is not allowed in the
                  statement part of a for-statement because a object
                  code contains a go-to jump to a do-loop.

<while statement>:= while <logical expression> ; <statement>

         ex.      WHILE I < N ! ^ ERROR ;
                  FORMAT READ:10 ERRFLAG = ERROR I, W ; ( I5,5E15.5)

<until statement>:= until <logical expression> ; <statement>

         ex.      UNTIL I >= N ; ( I = I + I; SUM = SUM + I )

<exec statement> := EXEC <procedure name>

         ex.      EXEC  SCAN ALL POSSIBLE CONFIGURATIONS

<procedure name> := <<words written in capital letters and symbol "_"
                      or words separated by a single blank>>

         ex.  1   SCAN ALL POSSIBLE CONFIGURATIONS

         ex.  2   SCAN_ALL_POSSIBLE_CONFIGURATIONS

<exit statement> := EXIT [ null ! <statement> ]

         note 1   EXIT causes the quitting of the execution of the
                  current block. If it appears in the outermost block,
                  the effect is euivalent to the RETURN statement, and
                  the use of EXIT instead of RETURN is recommended.

         note 2   An optional statement, if it exists, is executed
                  before the exit from the current block.

         note 3   The following two programs are similar, but give
                  different program controls.

                      program 1            program 2
                   DO WHILE A           WHILE A
                      .....             DO .....
                      .....                .....
                      IF X THEN EXIT       IF X THEN EXIT
                      .....                .....
                      END                  END
                   H = .....            H = .....

                  When the condition X is met, the control goes to the
                  statement H = .... in program 1, whereas the condition
                  A in the WHILE statement is tested in program 2.  If A
                  is true, DO block is again executed in this case.

         ex.      IF ERROR
                  THEN  EXIT  ' ERROR OCCURED WHILE READING DATA '

<read-write statement>
                 := [ FORMAT ! BINARY ] [ READ ! WRITE ! PUNCH ]
                    [ null ! : <<unit number>> ]
                    [ null ! ENDFLAG = <<simple logical var. name>> ]
                    [ null ! ERRFLAG = <<simple logical var. name>> ]
                    [ null ! IOSTAT = <<simple character var. name>> ]
                    <<input output list>>
                    [ null ! ; ( <<format specification>> ) ]

<list statement> := LIST [ null ! -1 ! -2 ! -3 ]  <statement>

         note     Three printout units are provided for <put statement>.
                  Their dd names are FT06F001, FT07F001 and FT08F001.
                  The record sizes are 132, 80 and 132 respectively.
                  Unit numbers can be specified by LIST ( or LIST-1 )
                  LIST-2 and LIST-3 respectively. Unit specification is
                  valid only in the following statement. After 
                  execution of this statement, the outer specification
                  is restored.  Default unit no. in the outermost
                  block is 1. For example,
                  
                                     Printout Unit no.
                PROGRAM MAIN1
                BEGIN
                   .....
                   PUT .....               1   ( default unit )
                   .....
                   LIST-2  DO
                      .....
                      'xxxxx'              2
                      PUT .....            2
                      .....
                      LIST   DO
                         .....
              a)         CALL SUB
                         PUT .....         1
                         'xxxxx'           1
                         .....
                         END
              b)      CALL SUB
                      .....
                      PUT .....            2
                      END
                   PUT .....               1   ( default unit )
                   .....
                   END

                SUBROUTINE SUB
                BEGIN
                   PUT .....               2  when called from a)
                   .....                   1  when called from b)
                   .....
                   LIST-3 PUT .....        3
                   .....
                   END

         ex.     LIST  CALL PRTHD ( TITLE )

<do block>       := DO <block>

         ex.      FOR I = 1, N ; A(I) =  0

<do for block>   := DO FOR <<do loop specification>> ; <block>

         ex.     DO FOR KR = 1, NS
                    1L; 5X; '+'; FOR KC = 1, NS; '---+'
                    1L; 5X; 'I';
                    FOR KC = 1, NS
                    IF POS(KR)=KC THEN ' Q I'; ELSE '   I'
                    END

<do while block> := DO WHILE <logical expression> ; <block>

         ex.      DO WHILE  KX <= NS
                     POS(1) = KX/2 + 1
                     N      = 1
                     EXEC   SCAN ALL POSITIONS IN THE NEXT ROW
                     KX     = KX + 2
                     END

<do until block> := DO UNTIL <logical expression> ; <block>

<do case arithmetic block>
                 := DO CASE OF <<simple variable>> ;
                    <arithmetic conditional statements> ;
                    <do case ending>

         ex.      DO CASE OF IITYP
                     *  F1 = -FT              ; /  when IITYP < 2
                     2: F1 =  FT              ; /  when IITYP = 2
                     *  (F1 = -FT;  F2 =  FT) ; /  when IITYP > 2
                     END OF DO CASE    ; / "OF DO CASE" after END is --
                                            not processed

<do case character block>
                 := DO CASE OF <<variable>> ; CHARACTER ;
                    <character conditional statements>;
                    <do case ending>

         ex.        DO CASE OF SYMB ; CHARACTER
                     CHARACTER
                    'DO ':  EXEC DO_X_STATEMENT
                    'IF ':  DO
                            NST = NST + 1
                            CALL STACK (NST)
                            *****
                            *****
                            END
                     OTHERWISE EXEC SIMPLE_STATEMENT

<do case logical block>
                 := DO CASE LOGICAL
                    <logical conditional statements> ;
                    <do case ending>

         ex.        DO CASE LOGICAL
                       CHRSTR: DO CASE OF NCHAR ;/ when QHRSTR is true /
                                  *  EXEC  SIMPLE_STATEMENT
                                  2: DO CASE OF SYMB
                                     *****
                                  OTHERWISE DO CASE OF SYMB
                                     *****
                                     OTHERWISE EXEC SKIP_LINE
                       QTSTR:  EXEC PUT_STRING ; / when QTSTR is true /
                       INTSTR: DO  ***** END
                       SYMB (1) = EXCSYM: EXEC EXEC_STATEMENT
                       SYMB (1) = SEMC:  ( EXEC W_CONTINUE; CALL GETS )
                       SYMB (1) = EQSYM: DO ***** END
                       SYMB (1) = LPAR:  EXEC DO_X_STATEMENT
                       OTHERWISE EXEC SKIP_LINE

<do case ending> := [ END ! OTHERWISE <statement> ]

         note     OTHERWISE means the following statement is executed
                  when none of the conditions are satisfied in the
                  conditional statements.

         ex.     DO CASE OF K
                    *         CALL SUB ;   /*      K < 3
                    3:
                    5:
                    7:        CALL SUB1;   /*      K = 3,5 or 7
                    *         CALL SUB2;   /*      7 < K < 11
                    11:       CALL SUB3;   /*      K = 11
                    OTHERWISE CALL SUB4;   /*      (K = 4 or 6 )
                                           /*      or K > 11

<arithmetic conditional statements>
                 := <a. c. st's> <arithmetic conditional statement>

<character conditional statements>
                 := <c. c. st's> <character conditional statement>

<logical conditional statements>
                 := <l. c. st's> <logical conditional statement>

<arithmetic conditional statement>
                 := <arithmetic conditions> <statement>

<character conditional statement>
                 := <character conditions> <statement>

<logical conditional statements>
                 := <logical conditions> <statement>

<arithmetic conditions>
                 := [ * ! <<arithmetic expression>> : ]
                  ! <arithmetic conditions> ;
                    [ * ! <<arithmetic expression>> : ]

         note     We show some examples of the conditions and their
                  meanings.

     arithmetic condition       meaning

        *         the value of the variable ( var ) isgreater than the
                  range specified in the previous arithmetic conditional
                  statement ( max-prev ) and less than the range specifi
                  specified in the following statement ( min-next ):
                  i. e.  max-prev < var < min-next

        3:        var = 3

        3:; 7:    var = 3 or 7

        * ; K+1:  max-prev < var <= K+1

        7:; *     7 <= var < min-next

<character conditions>
                 := [ * ! <<variable>> : ! <<character constant>> : ]
                  ! <character conditions> ;
                    [ * ! <<variable>> : ! <<character constant>> : ]

         note     The meaning of the character condition is almost the
                  same as the arithmetic conditions. The character strin
                  string is compared following the internal code of the
                  machine. The variable to be compared should be an
                  array of integer * 4 and the last column should
                  contain the binary "zero".

     character condition       meaning

        *         the value of the variable ( var ) isgreater than the
                  range specified in the previous character conditional
                  statement ( max-prev ) and less than the range specifi
                  specified in the following statement ( min-next ):
                  i. e.  max-prev < var < min-next

     'FORTRAN ':  var = 'FORTRAN '

     'READ ':
     'WRITE ':    var = 'READ ' or 'WRITE '

     *
     'WRITE ':    max-prev < var <=  'WRITE '

     'WRITE ':
     *            'WRITE ' <= var < min-next

<logical conditions>
                 := <logical expression> :
                  ! <logical conditions> ; <logical expression> :

         note     If two or more two logical expessions are specified,
                  those conditions are "or" concatinated.

     logical condition       meaning

       I < K :            I .LT. K

       J = K :
       J = L :            J = K or L

<list specification>
                 := <<integer constant>> [ P ! L ! X ! C ]

         note     P   for a new page
                  L   for line feed
                  X   for skipping columns from the current position
                  C   for specifying the column position

         ex.      1P  Eject a page
                  3L  Feeding three lines ( two blank lines left )
                  5X  Skip 5 columns. Already written text is untatched
                  33C Set at the 33-th columns

                  ( 1L )
                  'abcd'; 10C; 'efgh'; 7C; PUT K I5; 6X; 'xyz'; 1L
                    will print ( assuming K = 9999 )

                 column   1234567890123456789012
                         abcd    9999gh    xyz

<put statement>  := [ PUT <<simple or array variable>> [ I ! C ]
                       [ <<integer constant>> ! <<variable>> ] ]
                   ! [ null ! PUT ] <<character constant>>

         note     I   printing as a integer
                  C   printing as a character string
                  [ <<integer constant>> ! <<variable>> ]
                      for the field length

         ex.      PUT K I5    print K as an integer ( field size 5 )

                  PUT SYMBOL C LENGTH
                              print SYMBOL as a character string of
                              length LENGTH

                  '-----'     will print -----
                  PUT '-----' will also print -----

<variable modification>
                 :=  = <<simple variable>>
                     <<the rest of an arithmetic expression>>

         note     This statement may be conveniently used to add /
                  subtruct a certain number.

         ex.      = ISTACK + 1  means  ISTACK = ISTACK + 1

                  = K * 2       means  K      = K * 2

<fortran statement>
                 := FORTRAN
                    <<any fortran statement with or without a statement
                    number>>

         ex.      FORTRAN WRITE (*,FMT='(10I5)') JJ

                  FORTRAN 300  GO TO 310

<do fortran block>
                 := DO FORTRAN
                    <<a group of any fortran statements separated by
                      semicolon, with or without a statement number>>
                    END

         ex.       DO FORTRAN
                     IF ( NDIM .EQ. 1 ) THEN
                        SWGHT =  TR (1) * 2.0D0 ; GO TO 10
                     ELSE IF ( NDIM .EQ. 2 ) THEN
                        SWGHT =  TR(1) + TR(2)
                        GO TO 20
                        END IF
                     EMIN =   0 ; EMAX = 100
                     END


   "DO" and ";END" pair in the above syntax may be replaced by
the left and the right parentheses "(" and ")".


         ex.        ( for I = 1, N;  A (I) = C * B (I); B (I) = 0.0D0)
                    is euivalent to
                       DO FOR I = 1, N
                          A (I) = C * B (I)
                          B (I) = 0.0D0
                          END

4.  Macro Substitutions

<macro string definition>
                 := %<macro identifier> <macro text> %;

<macro identifier>
                 := %<<identifier>>

<macro text>     := <<a character string which contains no percent
                    symbol exept used as a part of a macro identifier>>

     Previously defined macro identifier in the text is replaced by the
     corresponding macro text before processing.

         ex. 1
                  %%SUBJECT HE%             ; / macro definitions
                  %%PREDICATE %VERB %COMPLEMENT.%
                               / note that macro identifier appears --
                                 in the macro text.
                  %%VERB IS%
                  %%COMPLEMENT A COMPOSER%

                  '%SUBJECT %PREDICATE'        ; / text /

                  %%VERB WILL BECOME%   ; / macro definition /

                  '%SUBJECT %PREDICATE'        ; / text /

           The above text is equivalent to

                  'HE IS A COMPOSER.'          ;

                  'HE WILL BECOME A COMPOSER.' ;

         ex. 2
                  %%STACK LEVEL = LEVEL + 1; ASTACK(LEVEL) =%
                  %%UNSTACK = ASTACK(LEVEL); LEVEL = LEVEL - 1%
                  %STACK BB
                  CALL F(BB)
                  XX %UNSTACK;

           is equivalent to

                  LEVEL = LEVEL + 1; ASTACK(LEVEL) =BB
                  CALL F(BB)
                  XX= ASTACK(LEVEL); LEVEL = LEVEL - 1


5.  Sample Programs

5.1  N-Queen Program

   A sample FPL program is given here. The program solves N-queens
   problem.
   
5.1.1  FPL source program
  
  process  headder nqueen
  program nqueen

  integer      --
  n,           --  current row of the queen.
  ns,          --  size of the board (default  8).
  tsol,        --  total number of solutions (default 10).
  pos(20),     --  column position of the queen in each row.
  iq9999 /0/,  --  stack pointer for recursive call proc.
  iq9998(0:300);/  stack for return address

  begin
   4c; 'enter board size and number of solutions: '; 1l
   fortran read(*,*) ns,tsol
   if  ns <= 1     then  ns = 8
   if  ns > 20 then exit do 
    'given board size ('; put ns i5 ;
                  ' ) is too large. ( max = 20 )' ; end
   if  tsol= 0     then  tsol = 10
   else if tsol > ns then tsol = ns
   3l;10c; put ns i3; '-queens problem'; 1l
  isol = 0
    kx =  1
    while  kx <= ns
    do
      pos(1) = kx/2 + 1
      n = 1
      exec  scan all positions in the next row
      = kx + 2
      end
    2l; for k=5,ns; '    '; '  end of program N-queen'
    end

   procedure scan all positions in the next row ; recursive
   if isol >= tsol then exit
     = n + 1
     pos (n) = 0
     until pos (n) = ns
   do pos (n) = pos (n) + 1
     kmax = n - 1
   for k = 1, kmax
   if pos(n) = pos(k) ! iabs (pos(n) - pos(k)) = n-k then exit
   if n < ns
   then  exec scan all positions in the next row
   else  exec display chess board
   end
   = n - 1
    end

   procedure   display chess board
    = isol + 1
    3l; '   solution'; put isol i4; 1l
    1l; '   +--'; for kc = 1, ns; '----'; '-+'
      do for kr = 1, ns
      1l; '   | +'; for kc = 1, ns; '---+'; ' |'
      1l; '   | |';
      for kc = 1, ns; if pos(kr)=kc then ' Q |'; else '   |'; ' |'
     end
      1l; '   | +'; for kc = 1, ns; '---+'; ' |'
    1l; '   +--'; for kc = 1, ns; '----'; '-+'
   end

5.1.2 Compile source list

    FPL90 compile list is given below.  Note the source indentation
in the compile list.  The number in the first four column positions
indicates corresponding card number of the object fortran code.


-NQUEEN-               FPL V(LAHEY286 11/14/89) OS/2-RMF 89-11-14 P  1
      process  headder nqueen
    1 program nqueen
 
      integer      --
    3 n,           --  current row of the queen.
      ns,          --  size of the board (default  8).
      tsol,        --  total number of solutions (default 10).
      pos(20),     --  column position of the queen in each row.
      iq9999 /0/,  --  stack pointer for recursive call proc.
      iq9998(0:300);/  stack for return address
 
    3 begin
    5    4c; 'enter board size and number of solutions: '; 1l
    7    fortran read(*,*) ns,tsol
    9    if  ns <= 1     then  ns = 8
   11    if  ns > 20 then exit do
   12       'given board size ('; put ns i5 ;
   14       ' ) is too large. ( max = 20 )' ; end
   16    if  tsol= 0     then  tsol = 10
   18    else if tsol > ns then tsol = ns
   22    3l;10c; put ns i3; '-queens problem'; 1l
   27    isol = 0
   28    kx =  1
   29    while  kx <= ns
   30    do
   30       pos(1) = kx/2 + 1
   31       n = 1
   32       exec  scan all positions in the next row
   34       = kx + 2
   34       end
   36    2l; for k=5,ns; '    '; '  end of program N-queen'
   40    end
 
   43 procedure scan all positions in the next row ; recursive
   46    if isol >= tsol then exit
   48    = n + 1
   49    pos (n) = 0
   51    until pos (n) = ns
   52    do pos (n) = pos (n) + 1
   53       kmax = n - 1
   54       for k = 1, kmax
   55       if pos(n) = pos(k) ! iabs (pos(n) - pos(k)) = n-k then exit
   60       if n < ns
   60       then  exec scan all positions in the next row
   63       else  exec display chess board
   67       end
   69    = n - 1
   69    end
 
   73 procedure   display chess board
   74    = isol + 1
   75    3l; '   solution'; put isol i4; 1l
   79    1l; '   +--'; for kc = 1, ns; '----'; '-+'
   85    do for kr = 1, ns
   86       1l; '   | +'; for kc = 1, ns; '---+'; ' |'
   92       1l; '   | |';
   94       for kc = 1, ns; if pos(kr)=kc then ' Q |'; else '   |'; ' |'
